.Dd July 12, 2026
.Dt NG_HCI_VIRT 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ng_hci_virt
.Nd virtual HCI controller netgraph node type
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In netgraph/bluetooth/include/ng_hci_virt.h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
netgraph node type is a software stand-in for a physical Bluetooth
controller.
It presents the identical upstream contract as the
.Xr ng_ubt 4
USB controller driver \(em a single hook that connects to the
.Xr ng_hci 4
node's
.Dq drv
hook and carries type-prefixed HCI packets in both directions \(em so the
Bluetooth stack
.Pq Xr ng_hci 4 , Xr ng_l2cap 4 , Xr blued 8
attaches to it exactly as it would to a real adapter, with no hardware
present.
.Pp
Each node is bridged to userspace through a per-instance cloning character
device,
.Pa /dev/vhci Ns Ar N .
The kernel performs no HCI parsing: the
.Dq drv Ns -hook
wire format (a one-byte packet-type indicator followed by the HCI payload)
is delivered verbatim.
Data arriving down the hook from
.Xr ng_hci 4
is host-to-controller traffic
.Pq HCI Command, ACL, SCO or ISO
and is queued for
.Xr read 2 ;
data written with
.Xr write 2
is controller-to-host traffic
.Pq HCI Event, ACL, SCO or ISO
and is forwarded up the hook.
.Pp
Nodes are created and destroyed through the
.Pa /dev/vhci
control device with the
.Dv VHCI_CREATE
and
.Dv VHCI_DESTROY
.Xr ioctl 2
requests; a created node is named
.Dq vhci Ns Ar N .
The
.Xr vhcitool 8
utility drives this interface and runs a controller emulator on the
userspace side.
.Sh HOOKS
.Bl -tag -width ".Va hook"
.It Va hook
The single controller hook.
It is connected to the
.Dq drv
hook of an
.Xr ng_hci 4
node, typically with
.Dq Li "mkpeer vhci Ns Ar N Ns : hci hook drv" .
.El
.Sh CONTROL MESSAGES
This node type supports only the generic control messages.
Instance lifecycle is managed through the
.Pa /dev/vhci
control device rather than netgraph control messages.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa /dev/vhci Ns Ar N" -compact
.It Pa /dev/vhci
control device
.Pq Dv VHCI_CREATE , Dv VHCI_DESTROY
.It Pa /dev/vhci Ns Ar N
per-node HCI packet endpoint
.El
.Sh SHUTDOWN
A node shuts down when it is destroyed through
.Dv VHCI_DESTROY ,
when the module is unloaded, or when it receives a
.Dv NGM_SHUTDOWN
control message.
Destruction fails with
.Er EBUSY
while
.Pa /dev/vhci Ns Ar N
is open.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr netgraph 4 ,
.Xr ng_hci 4 ,
.Xr ng_l2cap 4 ,
.Xr ng_ubt 4 ,
.Xr blued 8 ,
.Xr vhcitool 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Kory Heard
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
The
.Pa /dev/vhci
control device is restricted to the super-user
.Pq Dv PRIV_DRIVER .
A virtual controller injects HCI events directly into the kernel Bluetooth
stack and must be trusted accordingly.
